Use the following as the master implementation prompt for Gemini Argon. It instructs the AI to transform your existing RTS game into a highly detailed, realistic, procedurally generated world with dynamically modeled structures, terrain, infrastructure, and environmental simulation.

# MASTER DEVELOPMENT DIRECTIVE: REALISTIC PROCEDURAL WORLD GENERATION AND ENVIRONMENTAL SIMULATION FOR AN ADVANCED RTS GAME

You are acting as a senior game engine architect, procedural world-generation engineer, technical artist, 3D environment artist, simulation engineer, and gameplay systems developer.

Your task is to significantly upgrade the existing RTS game into a highly immersive, realistic, detailed, living, and dynamically generated real-time strategy environment.

The game is inspired by the environmental depth, strategic complexity, battlefield scale, and world-building of StarCraft and other advanced RTS titles. However, do not simply reproduce their assets or visual styles. Develop an original visual identity and environmental simulation system.

This is not a request to create a static demonstration, concept scene, or collection of decorative objects. Implement a complete, integrated, procedural environment-generation system that works with the existing game architecture.

The resulting world must look and behave like an actual geographical location with natural landscapes, settlements, infrastructure, industrial complexes, military installations, abandoned locations, environmental activity, and believable battlefield destruction.

==================================================
1. CRITICAL DEVELOPMENT REQUIREMENTS
==================================================

STRICTLY FOLLOW THESE REQUIREMENTS:

1. Do not create environments using primitive shapes as final building models.
2. Do not represent houses, factories, military installations, warehouses, towers, bridges, or other structures using combinations of basic cubes, spheres, cylinders, or flat geometric placeholders.
3. Do not use simplistic box-shaped buildings with colored surfaces as substitutes for actual architectural models.
4. Do not create a world consisting of flat terrain with randomly scattered decorative objects.
5. Do not hardcode a limited collection of building arrangements and repeatedly reuse them throughout the map.
6. Do not generate identical buildings with only different colors or scales.
7. Do not use untextured geometry.
8. Do not use flat, unrealistic, excessively saturated materials.
9. Do not sacrifice environmental detail merely to make implementation easier.
10. Do not replace existing gameplay systems unnecessarily.
11. Do not remove existing mechanics, controls, unit systems, AI behavior, resource systems, camera controls, or user interface features.
12. Do not create fake environmental interactions that are purely visual.
13. Do not generate a single predetermined map and describe it as procedural generation.
14. Do not leave important systems as TODO comments, placeholders, mockups, or unimplemented functions.

Use actual 3D mesh geometry, realistic materials, physically based rendering, appropriate textures, procedural modeling, and environmental simulation.

Preferred technologies:
- Three.js for rendering and scene management.
- WebGL or WebGPU where supported.
- Procedural mesh generation.
- BufferGeometry for custom-generated geometry.
- InstancedMesh for repeated environmental assets.
- GLTF/GLB models where appropriate.
- PBR materials.
- Procedural texture generation.
- Noise-based terrain generation.
- Physically based lighting.
- Spatial partitioning and level-of-detail systems.

You may introduce additional libraries if they materially improve the implementation.

If the existing project already uses another rendering engine or framework, evaluate its architecture before introducing Three.js. Avoid creating competing rendering systems.

