23. THREE.JS IMPLEMENTATION STANDARDS
==================================================

Use Three.js properly.

Recommended techniques:

- BufferGeometry for custom architectural and terrain meshes.
- ShaderMaterial for specialized environmental effects.
- MeshStandardMaterial or MeshPhysicalMaterial for PBR rendering.
- TextureLoader for external textures.
- DataTexture for generated procedural textures.
- InstancedMesh for vegetation and repeated environmental details.
- LOD for distant objects.
- Raycaster for appropriate environmental interaction.
- SkeletonUtils for animated imported models when necessary.
- GLTFLoader for compatible detailed 3D assets.
- KTX2Loader for compressed textures where supported.
- DRACOLoader for compressed geometry where appropriate.

Use actual mesh construction for:

- Roofs.
- Walls.
- Foundations.
- Doors.
- Window frames.
- Structural supports.
- Bridges.
- Towers.
- Industrial equipment.
- Terrain formations.

Use physically plausible material properties.

Create reusable geometry-generation functions with configurable parameters.

Ensure geometry has correct normals, UV coordinates, material groups, and collision representations.

Avoid creating excessive draw calls.

Use shared materials and geometry only where appropriate.

Do not share mutable geometry between objects when individual destruction or deformation is required.

